"Partitions concentric rings into angular spans that show a hierarchy’s additive composition by depth.\n\nUse when: A shallow hierarchy needs a compact overview of branching and relative shares. Readers can follow ancestry through aligned angular sectors.\n\nAvoid when: Long labels, deep hierarchies, or precise leaf comparisons dominate the task.\n\nVisual grammar: Ring position encodes hierarchy depth. Angular span encodes subtree value relative to the whole; containment through shared angles encodes ancestry.\n\nBuild: Validate a rooted hierarchy and calculate additive subtree values. Allocate angles to children within each parent span and depths to rings. Label only wide sectors, reserving external text or a key for narrow ones.\n\nCheck: Check child angular spans exactly fill their parent’s span. Verify equal values have equal angles at the same depth. State that sector area changes with radius and is not the primary value encoding.\n\nPitfalls: Outer-ring sectors look larger in area even when their angles match inner sectors. Prefer an icicle chart when circular labels require crossing leaders or excessive rotation.\n\nExample: Show synthetic departmental spending by division and team over three rings, with angular shares computed from leaf budgets.\n\nAlternatives: Icicle chart, Treemap, Circle-packing chart."
